# shade-tree: hardened torrc reference fragment.
#
# This is a REFERENCE, not wired into bootstrap.sh. bootstrap.sh writes the
# minimal working config (/etc/tor/torrc.d-shade-tree: two HiddenServiceDir blocks +
# HiddenServicePoWDefensesEnabled 1). The lines below are the safe extra subset
# you can layer on. Copy the SERVER block onto the droplet's torrc.d-shade-tree, and
# the CLIENT block onto the laptop/client tor. See docs/TOR-HARDENING.md for the
# reasoning and for the options deliberately left out (vanguards, client-auth).
#
# Legend:
#   [S]      server-side (droplet running the onion services)
#   [C]      client-side (laptop dialing the fleet)
#   [S-POW]  server-side AND requires a pow-capable tor build (official Tor
#            Project apt package has it; the Homebrew bottle does NOT -- the PoW
#            code is GPL and the bottle is BSD-only)
#   [VERIFY] confirm the option exists in YOUR tor version before relying on it


# ============================================================================
# SERVER SIDE  (droplet: /etc/tor/torrc.d-shade-tree, alongside the HiddenServiceDir
# blocks that bootstrap.sh already writes)
# ============================================================================

# [S] Drop privileges. The apt tor package already runs as debian-tor via its
# systemd unit; set this only if you launch tor yourself. Harmless to state.
# User debian-tor

# [S] Tor's own seccomp2 syscall sandbox (Linux/x86_64 only). Strong defense in
# depth around the tor process. CAVEATS: once enabled you cannot live-reload a
# torrc change that needs a new syscall or a new file path -- you must fully
# restart tor. Test a restart before trusting it in production.
Sandbox 1

# --- PoW DoS defense --------------------------------------------------------
# bootstrap.sh already sets HiddenServicePoWDefensesEnabled 1 per service. The
# two knobs below tune the request queue that the PoW gate feeds. They only take
# effect on a pow-capable build. Raise them under active flooding; see the doc.

# [S-POW][VERIFY] Steady-state rate the onion dequeues rendezvous requests from
# the PoW-priority queue (requests/sec). Higher = serve more real clients per
# second under load, at more CPU. Verify the exact name/units for your version.
# HiddenServicePoWQueueRate 250

# [S-POW][VERIFY] Max burst the queue absorbs above the steady rate. Raise with
# QueueRate when legit demand is spiky. Verify against your tor version.
# HiddenServicePoWQueueBurst 2500

# [S][VERIFY] Optional stream caps per rendezvous circuit -- caps a single
# client's fan-out. Close the circuit if exceeded. Tune to your real per-client
# concurrency. Left commented; verify names against your tor version.
# HiddenServiceMaxStreams 32
# HiddenServiceMaxStreamsCloseCircuit 1

# NOTE (server-side client-auth) is intentionally NOT here. For a PRIVATE fleet
# you would add an authorized_clients/ subdir under each HiddenServiceDir (v3
# mechanism), NOT the old v2 "HiddenServiceAuthorizeClient" torrc line. It is
# off by design: shade-tree gates at the app layer (zk reputation), which is the whole
# point. See docs/TOR-HARDENING.md section 3 before enabling.


# ============================================================================
# CLIENT SIDE  (laptop tor; compare tor/torrc.client in this repo)
# ============================================================================

# [C] Per-request circuit isolation. shade-tree rotates gateways per request; these
# flags make tor also use a SEPARATE circuit per distinct destination and per
# distinct SOCKS auth, so two requests do not share one exit-less path by
# accident. Ties directly to the per-request rotation goal.
#   IsolateDestAddr    new circuit per distinct destination address (.onion)
#   IsolateDestPort    new circuit per distinct destination port
#   IsolateSOCKSAuth   new circuit per distinct SOCKS username/password pair
#                      (the client can pass a per-request tag as SOCKS auth to
#                       force a fresh circuit on demand)
SocksPort 9260 IsolateDestAddr IsolateDestPort IsolateSOCKSAuth

# [C] How long a circuit keeps accepting NEW streams before tor rotates to a
# fresh one (seconds; default 600). Lower = fresher circuits, more build churn
# and more load on guards. Isolation flags above already fork per destination;
# lower this only if you also want time-based rotation within one destination.
# MaxCircuitDirtiness 600

# [C] Client-auth private keys (only if the SERVER enabled v3 client-auth).
# Point tor at a dir of "<onion>.auth_private" files. Off by default in shade-tree.
# ClientOnionAuthDir /var/lib/tor/shade-tree-onion-auth
